#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119556 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95f2e191-0760-5cb8-b55a-cd2955150e82" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equation of an Osculating Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "b1d808a6-49e1-54ae-88c7-36c42f93e179" :question/math-academy-id "e-3060" :question/problem "![](images/19/1990d7cb1625ca97f10debf834883f6e434473f154cf9683db60ee7c4a69c83e.png)\n\nFind a parametric equation of the osculating plane for the curve  $\\mathbf r(t)$ at the point where $t=1,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle -2t , \\: t^2 , \\: \\dfrac{1}{3}t^3 \\right\\rangle, \\qquad\n\\mathbf T(1) = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left\\langle -2 , 2 , 1 \\right\\rangle, \\qquad\n\\mathbf N(1) = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left\\langle 2 , 1 , 2\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The osculating plane passes through the point $P,$ which is at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r(1).$ The vectors $\\mathbf T(1)$ and $\\mathbf N(1)$ are parallel to this plane and are not collinear, so a parametric equation for the plane can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(s,u) = \\mathbf r(1) + s \\, \\mathbf T(1) + u \\, \\mathbf N(1), \\qquad s,u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing $\\mathbf r(1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨-2(1),\\,(1)^{2},\\,\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}⟩=⟨-2,1,\\frac{1}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a parametric equation for the osculating plane at the point $\\left(-2,1,\\dfrac 1 3 \\right)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s,u) & =r(1)+s\\,T(1)+u\\,N(1) \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,\\frac{1}{3}⟩+s\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,2,1⟩+u\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨2,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-6-2s+2u,\\,3+2s+u,\\,1+s+2u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119557 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68feb21c-0d21-5889-8d1e-2eb4b27382af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of an Osculating Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "0ecbb0ee-5559-55c3-9aba-0056288a6c67" :question/math-academy-id "e-3061" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ at the point where $t=0,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k) \\\\ N(t) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡tk).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane, we need to compute the position vector $\\mathbf p$ and a vector $\\mathbf n$ that is perpendicular to the osculating plane. \n\nThe position vector of the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p} = \\mathbf{r}(0) = \\cos{0}\\,\\mathbf i+\\cos{0}\\,\\mathbf j+\\sqrt{2}\\sin{0}\\, \\mathbf k = \\langle 1, 1, 0 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nTo compute the perpendicular vector, we first need to compute the unit tangent $\\mathbf T(0)$ and the principal normal $\\mathbf N(0)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\,j+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\,k)=k \\\\ N(0) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\,j+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0k)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the cross product of these two vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =k\\times (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are interested only in the direction of the vector above, we can multiply it by a constant to yield a simpler vector that points in the same direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n} = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt 2}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\left(\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j\\right) =\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nFinally, let $\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ denote the position vector of a general point in the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $\\mathbf{n}$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $\\mathbf{p},$ its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,0⟩ & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\ 1\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y+0\\cdot z & =0 \\\\ x-y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/ad/ad22c676123844c2428febb34e0218cfcb05917154e8cff994f35fa4fbf68944.png)\n\n**Note:** Because our curve is a plane curve, it has the same osculating plane at each point. As shown above, the osculating plane coincides with the plane of the curve. \n\nThis result holds in general: any plane curve has the same osculating plane at each of its points (wherever the osculating plane is defined)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119558 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119742 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a445fa-d44b-574a-8369-b30cac3a3c96" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Shortest Distance Between an Osculating Plane and the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "7b4c22d8-65f0-5a5e-9eb5-754f778a7bf7" :question/math-academy-id "e-3062" :question/problem "![](images/46/46e9f26846b088dfe225b36b889c788ed847c08376295f02dc2c9fbaf72a1645.png)\n\nFind the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡6t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡6t,\\,8t⟩ \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡6t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡6t,\\,4⟩ \\\\ N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡6t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡6t,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane, we need to compute the position vector $\\mathbf p$ and a vector $\\mathbf n$ that is perpendicular to the osculating plane. \n\nThe position vector of the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p} = \\mathbf{r}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\left\\langle \\sin{\\left(6\\cdot\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)} , \\:  -\\cos{\\left(6\\cdot\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)}, \\: 8\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\right\\rangle  = \\left\\langle -1, 0, 2\\pi \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTo compute the perpendicular vector, we need to compute the unit tangent $\\mathbf T \\left( \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\right)$ and the principal normal $\\mathbf N \\left( \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\right) \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,4⟩=\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,-3,4⟩ \\\\ N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,0⟩=⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the cross product of these two vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,-3,4⟩\\times ⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\,j+3\\,k) \\\\  & =⟨0,\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are interested only in the direction of the vector above, we can multiply it by a constant to yield a simpler vector that points in the same direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n} = 5\\cdot \\left\\langle 0 , \\dfrac{4}{5} , \\dfrac{3}{5} \\right\\rangle = \\langle 0, 4, 3  \\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{|⟨-1,0,2\\pi ⟩\\cdot ⟨0,4,3⟩|}{‖⟨0,4,3⟩‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pi }{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95f2e191-0760-5cb8-b55a-cd2955150e82" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equation of an Osculating Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38c1e0ab-2828-5e9c-9df8-5e2c181ea0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,(s-u)\\sqrt{3},\\,(s+u)\\sqrt{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375827} {:answer/id #uuid "9e76d983-055b-554b-bc9b-36fe5b1ed5a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨-\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,1-\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,1+\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375828} {:answer/id #uuid "c3bd6cff-b279-5c03-a2c3-437153fecca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨-\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375829} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e799c3-9581-586b-8d38-bfd28a7079b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨1+\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,1-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375830} {:answer/id #uuid "267277fb-aa68-5bfb-abab-4bd34d2544b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,(s+u)\\sqrt{3},\\,(s-u)\\sqrt{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e76d983-055b-554b-bc9b-36fe5b1ed5a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨-\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,1-\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,1+\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375828} :answer-field/id #uuid "187786e6-deb0-5d64-8f2a-517c86ffee30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f4a870e-ec48-5855-9ff2-1d02c3e15a46" :question/math-academy-id "q-127278" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the osculating plane for the curve  $r(t)$ at the point where $t=0,$ given that\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,T(0)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟨-1,-1,1⟩,\\,N(0)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨0,-1,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The osculating plane passes through the point $P$, which is at the tip of the vector $r(0).$ The vectors $T(0)$ and $N(0)$ are parallel to this plane. Therefore, a parametric equation for the plane can be written as\n\n$$\nR(s,u)=r(0)+s\\,T(0)+u\\,N(0),\\,s,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩=⟨0,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, a parametric equation for the osculating plane at the point $(0,1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s,u) & =r(0)+s\\,T(0)+u\\,N(0) \\\\  & =⟨0,1,1⟩+s\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟨-1,-1,1⟩+u\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨0,-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,1-\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,1+\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68feb21c-0d21-5889-8d1e-2eb4b27382af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of an Osculating Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cce7a57-ec50-5b7d-a10d-d12d340f7ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375855} {:answer/id #uuid "0dd603f8-dae9-5232-88ef-942f6c34a5d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375856} {:answer/id #uuid "e673e372-7f75-50af-8159-f2472454cfed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+z-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375857} {:answer/id #uuid "98881dce-2ee9-5276-ac33-8fcb4aca837c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375858} {:answer/id #uuid "4a92762f-efaa-559b-abe8-23eb75919596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-z=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cce7a57-ec50-5b7d-a10d-d12d340f7ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375855} :answer-field/id #uuid "38507475-0514-5b9b-b6cc-a25300e0334e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5bbe6880-9115-5b6f-a9bb-cfd1553b146d" :question/math-academy-id "q-127295" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for a curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,1,2)$ where $t=\\pi ,$ given that\n\n$$\nT(\\pi )=i,\\,N(\\pi )=-k.\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(0,1,2)$ is $p=⟨0,1,2⟩.$\n\nTo find the equation of the osculating plane, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to it. To do that, we find the cross product of the unit tangent $T(1)$ and the principal normal $N(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\times N(1) & =i\\times (-k) \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our perpendicular vector $n$ is \n\n$$\nn=T(\\pi )\\times N(\\pi )=j=⟨0,1,0⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a general point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ & =⟨0,1,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\ x(0)+y(1)+z(0) & =0(0)+1(1)+2(0) \\\\ 0+y+0 & =0+1+0 \\\\ y-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68feb21c-0d21-5889-8d1e-2eb4b27382af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of an Osculating Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cecf739-72ba-5487-96f6-0db04f45b2ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375862} {:answer/id #uuid "a5405c60-125e-50c7-b4d5-cad7b7311891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y-z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375863} {:answer/id #uuid "077d07a8-a85d-5d26-b5b2-3cf85ef93746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+z-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375864} {:answer/id #uuid "e123dac7-b1cf-5d1d-a2fe-d98f864d5b00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375865} {:answer/id #uuid "5defad4f-2e3b-53f8-83af-3aa31470ddde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cecf739-72ba-5487-96f6-0db04f45b2ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375862} :answer-field/id #uuid "91d9bf77-7863-573d-8373-7f2526605345" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "569b65f9-8938-5c3e-b130-07826f3d58f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-127301" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=0,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2,\\,-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ N(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=0$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\ N(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\np=r(0)=⟨{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,2,\\,-{e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩=⟨0,2,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(0)$ and the principal normal $N(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\times N(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our perpendicular vector $n$ is \n\n$$\nn=T(0)\\times N(0)=⟨0,-1,0⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-1,0⟩ & =⟨0,2,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\ x(0)+y(-1)+z(0) & =0(0)+2(-1)+(-1)(0) \\\\ 0-y+0 & =0-2+0 \\\\ y-2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68feb21c-0d21-5889-8d1e-2eb4b27382af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of an Osculating Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ace2e9b8-b4bc-50c1-9470-dd8e5725a5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - z + 1 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117760} {:answer/id #uuid "dd53f8a3-bcdc-5471-b6b3-57135c6fe22e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + \\sqrt{3}z + 1 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117761} {:answer/id #uuid "f9ea83c4-540d-52bc-8391-3fedb3c5b181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - z + \\sqrt{3} = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117762} {:answer/id #uuid "2df87f7a-3a21-5ffe-98e7-9267bea982d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + \\sqrt{3}z - \\sqrt{3} = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117763} {:answer/id #uuid "386bc22b-0e66-583e-bf7b-8e7e29a8c9ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}x - z - \\sqrt{3} = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117764}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b1e11bc9-cec7-5abb-9e74-1d0988c65cb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117759}] :question/id #uuid "3005bcca-c33e-585c-8e84-018875446bff" :question/math-academy-id "q-33665" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t = 0$, given that\n\n$r(t)|= (\\cos t + t\\sin t)i + (\\sin t - t\\cos t)j + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}t^{2}k; T(t)|= \\frac{1}{2}(\\cos ti + \\sin tj + \\sqrt{3}k); N(t)|=-\\sin ti + \\cos tj$.\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68feb21c-0d21-5889-8d1e-2eb4b27382af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of an Osculating Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4d7080c-757d-574d-9c5e-449f54886537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + y - 1 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117796} {:answer/id #uuid "03e2fae3-e760-5b3c-ab84-86398e4af01e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117797} {:answer/id #uuid "23b2f010-802b-5c2a-9182-291b06411fe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - y - 1 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117798} {:answer/id #uuid "58057868-ead7-5d83-97df-83c0b24aa838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - y + 1 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117799} {:answer/id #uuid "79d77304-c402-53c7-8c19-15ac64b5ec7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x - y - 1 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117800}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b16a9277-3747-5cfc-a2ea-e503ba88803b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117795}] :question/id #uuid "857f2717-334c-550e-bce1-cd3a3ad47f82" :question/math-academy-id "q-33782" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for a curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(1, 0, 0)$ where $t = 1$, given that\n\n$T(1) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i + j), N(1) = k$.\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95f2e191-0760-5cb8-b55a-cd2955150e82" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equation of an Osculating Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a023a803-852d-5e5c-9ef4-3e04b220aa63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s, u) = \\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-\\sqrt{2}s, - π + u⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117928} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b15a77-f63c-5baf-b5a2-f124bb511c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s, u) = ⟨ - 2, 2π + \\sqrt{2}s,-\\sqrt{2}s⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117929} {:answer/id #uuid "aea4c972-4eb3-5063-ab50-2763290f949a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s, u) = \\frac{1}{2}⟨ - \\sqrt{2}s, 2π,-2 + 2u⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117930} {:answer/id #uuid "0fe818ed-f5d0-5fc3-a72d-cb9132f98c39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s, u) = ⟨ - 2 + 2u,-\\sqrt{2}s, \\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117931} {:answer/id #uuid "401e9e64-93ed-556c-9270-f68c84505d14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s, u) = \\frac{1}{2}⟨2π + \\sqrt{2}s,-2 + 2u,-\\sqrt{2}s⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117932}] :answer-field/id #uuid "37f3d36c-d151-51a5-9ff3-aa273e1e2fed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117927}] :question/id #uuid "091c6a72-5e82-5c94-a2c2-b3675bcf57e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-34082" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t = π$, given that\n\n$r(t) = ⟨t, \\cos t, \\sin t⟩, T(π) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1, 0,-1⟩, N(π) = ⟨0, 1, 0⟩$.\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95f2e191-0760-5cb8-b55a-cd2955150e82" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equation of an Osculating Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ec9aa97-f0f7-5a10-8249-361b901e466a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393746} {:answer/id #uuid "de4add72-0cd4-537b-80b7-0002434bbdf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,1-\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,1-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393747} {:answer/id #uuid "250743fd-4960-51b1-885b-3ed93deac3de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨1+3s,\\,2s+3u,\\,2s-3u⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393748} {:answer/id #uuid "07171637-65de-5c83-8235-9dc05a85624e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393749} {:answer/id #uuid "28f201ce-0d59-5c71-83a0-18e65a037a4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨2+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,1-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,3+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07171637-65de-5c83-8235-9dc05a85624e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393749} :answer-field/id #uuid "78aa28f8-2396-55a6-bbc4-e9955564a261" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf74894d-e339-586f-8261-369741108e3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-77651" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the osculating plane for the curve  $r(t)$ at the point where $t=0$, given that\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,T(0)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,\\,1,\\,1⟩,\\,N(0)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The osculating plane passes through the point that is at the tip of the vector $r(0).$ The unit tangent vector $T(0)$ and the principal normal vector $N(0)$ are parallel to this plane. Therefore, a parametric equation for the plane can be written as \n\n$$\nR(s,u)=r(0)+sT(0)+uN(0),\\,s,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{e}^{0},\\,{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,{e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩=⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, a parametric equation for the osculating plane at the point $(1,\\,0,\\,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s,u) & =r(0)+sT(0)+uN(0) \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩+s\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,\\,1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
